<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#pronoia">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pronoia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century–<a href="/years/1453">1453</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as pronoiai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pronoia, Greek for &quot;care&quot; or &quot;forethought&quot;, was a grant of state revenue in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, from the 11th century to the 15th. It transferred imperial fiscal rights, chiefly the taxes of cultivated land, usually for the holder's lifetime; the emperor could revoke it at will. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a> gave pronoiai to aristocratic officers instead of a salary, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>, who recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1261, let them be inherited. It carried no specific military obligation, unlike a western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> borrowed it for their own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timar</a> system.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>By the 11th century Byzantine aristocrats held honorific titles and power at the emperor's gift, and competition for them was fierce. The most desired grants involved governing and collecting taxes in parts of the empire. By the reign of Constantine IX, in the middle of the century, some aristocrats had begun to assert <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over regions, collecting taxes for themselves and often plotting rebellion.</p><p>In the late 11th century Alexius I tried to reform the aristocracy by distributing Roman territory among its members. This also removed them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and made it harder for them to challenge the emperor directly. Most of the pronoiai Alexius granted went to members of his own Comnenus family. His measure legitimized the holding of land by aristocrats and brought it under centralized state control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The limits of a pronoia were recorded in an imperial document called a praktika. Its holder was a pronoiarios, and those who worked the income stream, such as farmers on the land, were called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paroikoi">paroikoi</a>. The grant could be land, its money value or the income it produced, and could also cover water and fishing rights or customs collection. Several people might hold different rights to one piece of territory.</p><p>A pronoia gave the grantee possession, not ownership, which remained imperial. When monasteries or other institutions received grants, these were in effect permanent, since the institutions continued. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paroikoi">paroikoi</a> were not serfs as peasants were in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>: they owed no particular loyalty or service to the pronoiar, who was often not a native of the land granted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Comnenus</a> extended pronoiai to army officers in place of a regular salary, so that holders became something like tax collectors who kept part of what they raised. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204 the system continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-nicaea">Empire of Nicaea</a>, where John III Ducas Vatatzes gave pronoiai to the church and to noblewomen, which had not been done before.</p><p>Michael VIII Palaeologus, after recovering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1261, audited the pronoiai so that their values matched reduced conditions. Under the Palaeologan emperors pronoiars could more easily be organized into military units, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andronikos-ii-palaiologos">Andronicus II Palaeologus</a> used the money raised from them to finance expeditions against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. There were only a few thousand pronoiars, and the impoverished empire could not afford a full army or navy. Pronoiars began to extract rents from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paroikoi">paroikoi</a>. After the fall of Constantinople in 1453 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> kept their own version, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timar">timar</a> system, which they had borrowed from the Eastern Romans.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pronoia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Kamakura to the Tokugawa shogunates; to Zhou China, whose system of enfeoffment, fengjian, gave modern Chinese its word for feudal; to medieval Ethiopia; and to grants of revenue in return for service such as the Byzantine <b>pronoia</b>, the iqta of the Islamic world and the Mughal jagir.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Historians debate how far 10th-century stratiotai still fought in person; by the 11th century the duty had become a tax that paid for mercenaries, and under the Komnenoi officers were rewarded with grants of tax revenue (<b>pronoia</b>).</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
